config MESHTASTIC_PKI
	bool "PKC for direct messages (Curve25519/X25519 + AES-CCM)"
	depends on MESHTASTIC_NODEDB && MESHTASTIC_SETTINGS
	help
	  Implements Meshtastic public-key cryptography for direct messages,
	  interoperable with the reference firmware: an X25519 keypair (generated
	  once, persisted in the SecurityConfig NVS partition — survives reboot and
	  reflash), advertised via User.public_key, with AES-256-CCM DM
	  encrypt/decrypt (key = SHA256(ECDH shared secret)). Fixes both the
	  "PKI send failed, no public key" a peer sees and the NO_CHANNEL a peer
	  returns for our PSK-only DMs.

	  Requires PSA crypto: ECDH + ECC Montgomery-255 (key pair
	  generate/import/export + public key), AES, CCM and SHA-256. On the net
	  build the ECC/AES/SHA pieces come from the TLS overlay; the overlay adds
	  PSA_WANT_ALG_CCM.

config MESHTASTIC_PKI_SECRET_CACHE
	int "Peers whose derived PKC key is kept"
	depends on MESHTASTIC_PKI
	range 0 32
	default 8 if SOC_SERIES_NRF52
	default 10
	help
	  Every PKC frame needs SHA256(X25519(our private key, the peer's
	  public key)), and a direct message needs it three times for the same
	  peer (its decrypt, our ACK's encrypt, the decrypt of the ACK the
	  sender returns). Without a cache each is a full X25519: 115 ms on the
	  ESP32-S3 with PSA's software ECDH, on the mesh thread. This keeps the
	  derived keys of the most recently used peers, matched on the whole
	  public key, as the reference does since 2.8 (CryptoEngine
	  setCryptoSharedSecret: 10 entries, 8 on nRF52). 68 bytes each.
	  Emptied whenever our own keypair is (re)loaded. 0 derives every time.

choice MESHTASTIC_PKI_X25519
	prompt "X25519 implementation for the per-peer key"
	default MESHTASTIC_PKI_X25519_PSA
	depends on MESHTASTIC_PKI
	help
	  Which code computes X25519(our private key, the peer's public key)
	  for a PKC frame (agents-28qw). All three give the same bytes; they
	  differ in speed, flash and stack. Measure with `meshtastic sched
	  stats` (the PKI line) or the "pki" object of `meshtastic state`.
	  Our own public key is derived through PSA whichever is chosen (once
	  per boot).

config MESHTASTIC_PKI_X25519_PSA
	bool "PSA, tf-psa-crypto's generic elliptic-curve code"
	help
	  psa_raw_key_agreement on the builtin driver: the Montgomery ladder
	  over mbedTLS's general bignum code. 115 ms on the ESP32-S3.

config MESHTASTIC_PKI_X25519_EVEREST
	bool "PSA, with Project Everest's verified Curve25519"
	help
	  The same PSA call, routed by tf-psa-crypto to HACL* Curve25519 from
	  Project Everest (MBEDTLS_ECDH_VARIANT_EVEREST_ENABLED, set through
	  TF_PSA_CRYPTO_USER_CONFIG_FILE). Formally verified, constant time.
	  Apache-2.0 only, which suits this GPL-3 project. On a 32-bit core it
	  emulates its 128-bit multiplies (FStar_UInt128_extracted), so a
	  native_sim timing does not predict a board's.

config MESHTASTIC_PKI_X25519_REF10
	bool "ref10 field arithmetic (the vendored orlp/ed25519 fe.c)"
	help
	  RFC 7748's Montgomery ladder (src/crypto/x25519_ref10.c, ours) over
	  the ref10 field code already vendored for XEdDSA verification
	  (src/crypto/ed25519/fe.c, zlib). Constant time (conditional swaps).
	  Shares fe.c with MESHTASTIC_XEDDSA, so it costs little flash where
	  that is on.

endchoice

# The Everest switch is a tf-psa-crypto build option, not a Kconfig one: a user config
# header carries it (src/crypto/psa/meshtastic_psa_everest.h).
config TF_PSA_CRYPTO_USER_CONFIG_FILE
	default "meshtastic_psa_everest.h" if MESHTASTIC_PKI_X25519_EVEREST

config MESHTASTIC_NODE_ID_FROM_KEY
	bool "Derive the node ID from the identity public key (reference >= 2.8)"
	depends on MESHTASTIC_PKI
	select CRC
	help
	  Use crc32(X25519 public key) as the node ID, as the reference firmware
	  has since 2.8 (NodeDB::createNewIdentity, shipped with packet signing).
	  The id then commits to the key: nobody can claim an id without also
	  holding the key it was derived from, which is what makes trusting a
	  stranger's first signed NodeInfo safe.

	  MESHTASTIC_NODE_ID_SOURCE still supplies the PROVISIONAL id, used only
	  when no key is readable and none was stored. The adopted id is kept in
	  the unsealed "mtid" settings record so a lockdown-locked boot, which
	  cannot read the keypair, keeps the same id.

	  Turning this on CHANGES the id of an existing node once, on its first
	  boot, and every peer then holds the node under both ids until the old
	  entry is forgotten or ages out (the reference does the same). Keys do
	  not change, so the new id is predictable before flashing, and turning
	  it off again returns the node to its hardware id.
